-    ["/guides/", "Historical stock research guides", "Learn to compare stock prices with annual EPS, interpret free cash flow per share, and download historical chart data without signup.", `<h1>Historical stock research guides</h1><p>Use annual financial facts and historical stock charts to understand a business over time. These guides and the chart tool are free, with no account or subscription.</p><ul class="guide-list"><li><a href="/guides/stock-price-vs-earnings/">Compare stock price with earnings</a><p>Read the EPS chart, choose a multiple, and understand the formula reference.</p></li><li><a href="/guides/eps-vs-free-cash-flow-per-share/">EPS versus free cash flow per share</a><p>Understand the measures, their source filings, and when comparisons can mislead.</p></li><li><a href="/guides/export-stock-history-csv/">Download historical stock data as CSV</a><p>Select a company, metric, and window, then use the exported series in a spreadsheet.</p></li><li><a href="/guides/fiscal-year-dividend-yield/">Fiscal-year dividend yield</a><p>Calculate a historical year-end yield and distinguish it from trailing or forward yields.</p></li></ul><p>Start with ${companyLink}, or <a href="/">search for another US-listed company</a>. Coverage and dates vary by company.</p>`],
+    ["/guides/pe-ratio-history/", "Historical P/E ratios: annual basic and diluted EPS", "Explore free annual P/E ratio history, compare basic and diluted EPS, and understand adjusted prices, gaps, and reference lines.", `<h1>P/E ratio history explained</h1><p>Stock Value Lens provides free approximate fiscal-year-end price-to-earnings history on its existing stock pages and chart tool. Select basic EPS, diluted EPS, or both; optionally show 15×, 20×, and 30× comparison lines. No account is required.</p><p class="worked-formula">Annual P/E = fiscal-year-end close ÷ reported annual EPS</p><h2>Which price and earnings are used?</h2><p>Each observation matches a fiscal period end to the last positive split-only close on or before that date, no more than 14 days earlier. If unavailable, use a positive Stooq adjusted close in that interval and label the ratio approximate. Weekly sampling means the price date can precede the fiscal end. Basic and diluted ratios use the corresponding reported annual EPS for that same fiscal period. Diluted EPS reflects potential dilution; basic EPS uses the basic share base.</p><h2>Example</h2><p>If a year-end close is 100 and annual diluted EPS is 5, P/E is 20×. If basic EPS is 5.2, basic P/E is approximately 19.23×. These are hypothetical inputs, not company data.</p><h2>Use the chart and export</h2><ol><li>Open ${companyLink}, or search a ticker in the <a href="/">chart tool</a>.</li><li>Scroll to P/E ratio history and select the EPS basis. The chart follows the date range selected in the main chart.</li><li>Enable optional 15× / 20× / 30× lines. They are visual comparisons, not fair-value estimates or recommendations.</li><li>Use P/E CSV for both ratios, EPS inputs, price dates and types, and filing dates; use P/E PNG for the selected chart image.</li></ol><p>Company pages also contain the full stored annual P/E table with EPS filing links, available without JavaScript. Their Markdown alternatives contain the same derived observations. Coverage varies by company.</p><h2>Limitations</h2><p>This is annual fiscal-year-end P/E, not daily or weekly P/E, rolling TTM P/E, or analyst-estimate forward P/E. Annual facts can be reported or restated after the displayed fiscal end. The dataset applies supported later splits; adjusted prices can include dividend effects and differ from an actual unadjusted traded close. Price and EPS adjustment differences may affect comparability. Results are retrospective, not a reconstruction of information available to investors on each historical date.</p><p>Non-positive or missing EPS creates a gap rather than zero or a negative P/E. Missing, future, or stale prices are not substituted. Ratios are uncapped; very small positive EPS can produce extreme values. A period after the final stored price has no eligible ratio. Chart lines connect annual observations and do not represent intervening measurements. <a href="/sources/">Check source dates and limitations</a>.</p>${credit}`],
