     ["/guides/stock-price-vs-earnings/", "Compare historical stock price with earnings", "Use the free stock EPS chart to compare historical prices with annual earnings, choose a custom multiple, and understand the formula reference.", `<h1>Compare historical stock price with earnings</h1><p>A stock price chart shows what the market paid. Annual earnings per share (EPS) adds a record of the business's reported profit per share. Stock Value Lens lets you examine both in one historical chart, without signup.</p><h2>Open an annual EPS history chart</h2><ol><li>Open ${companyLink}, or search for a ticker in the <a href="/">chart tool</a>.</li><li>Select Diluted EPS. Basic EPS is also available where the company has supported annual observations.</li><li>Choose a timeframe or drag the range handles. The chart shows weekly prices and annual reported facts; it is not a quarterly or trailing-twelve-month earnings chart.</li><li>Review Annual metric change, then check the full annual table and its filing links. A company page can contain financial periods newer than the final price date.</li></ol><h2>Read the price and formula reference</h2><p>The historical price line uses the available price series. Stooq adjusted prices are labeled approximations where validated split-only closes are unavailable. The orange line multiplies positive annual EPS by a reference multiple, calculated from growth across the selected window or supplied by you.</p><p>Changing the multiple changes the reference, not the company's earnings. As a hypothetical illustration, EPS of 2 multiplied by 15 gives a reference of 30 in the same currency per share. This illustration is not an estimate of any company's fair value.</p><h2>Choose and inspect a multiple</h2><p>Leave Custom multiple blank for the growth-based formula or enter a positive multiple up to 60. The Annualized metric growth and Applied multiple cards show the values used for the current metric and window. Use Reset to restore the formula multiple. Changing the window can change both its growth calculation and reference line.</p><p><a href="/methodology/">Read the exact CAGR and reference-multiple formulas.</a> Missing or non-positive annual observations leave gaps in the reference line. A stock trading below it is not, by itself, evidence that the stock is undervalued.</p><h2>Respect the historical dates</h2><p>The chart uses retrospective information, including stored restated annual facts. It is not a point-in-time backtest, forecast, intrinsic-value calculation, or live quote service. Compare the displayed price date with the latest fiscal period before drawing conclusions.</p><p><a href="/guides/eps-vs-free-cash-flow-per-share/">Compare earnings with free cash flow per share</a> or <a href="/guides/export-stock-history-csv/">export the selected chart data</a>.</p>${credit}`],
     ["/guides/eps-vs-free-cash-flow-per-share/", "EPS versus free cash flow per share", "Compare annual earnings per share and free cash flow per share with free historical stock charts and source filings, including missing-data limits.", `<h1>EPS versus free cash flow per share</h1><p>Earnings per share and free cash flow per share describe different aspects of a business. Examining their annual histories can help you ask why reported profit and cash generation differ. Both chart views are free where supported company data is available.</p><h2>What annual EPS measures</h2><p>EPS expresses earnings attributable to common shareholders per share. Basic EPS uses the basic share base; diluted EPS reflects potential dilution under the applicable accounting rules. This tool uses reported SEC annual EPS facts and adjusts historical per-share observations for supported later stock-split facts. It does not synthesize missing EPS.</p><h2>How this tool calculates FCF per share</h2><p class="worked-formula">FCF per share = (operating cash flow − cash capital expenditure) ÷ annual diluted weighted-average shares</p><p>This is a derived measure using matched annual inputs, not a standardized GAAP figure. Company tables link the operating cash flow and capex filings. Separate share-denominator provenance is not included in the current public export. Read the <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> and check original filings before treating calculations from different services as directly comparable.</p><h2>Compare the histories</h2><ol><li>Open ${companyLink}, or choose a company from the <a href="/stocks/">directory</a>.</li><li>Select Diluted EPS and review its fiscal periods and filing sources.</li><li>Switch to FCF / share while keeping the same timeframe. A disabled metric means that the chart has no supported observations for it.</li><li>Use the full annual table to compare values for the same fiscal end. Check gaps and newer annual rows that fall outside the price-bounded chart.</li></ol><p>Working-capital changes, investment spending, non-cash expenses, and share-count changes can make the histories differ. A difference is a question to investigate in the filings, not an automatic quality score or buy/sell signal. Stock splits and restatements can also affect historical comparisons.</p><h2>When the comparison is unsuitable</h2><p>FCF as defined here may be unavailable or economically unsuitable for banks, REITs, and some other businesses. Do not substitute zero for unavailable observations. Percentage growth and CAGR are also difficult to interpret across losses or zero values; the chart formula uses positive endpoints and can leave gaps.</p><p>Both metrics are annual, not quarterly or trailing-twelve-month measures. Keep the fiscal dates and currency consistent. <a href="/guides/stock-price-vs-earnings/">Read the historical chart</a> or <a href="/guides/export-stock-history-csv/">download its CSV</a>.</p>${credit}`],
     ["/guides/export-stock-history-csv/", "Download historical stock EPS and cash flow data as CSV", "Download selected historical stock EPS or FCF per share chart data as a free CSV without signup, with instructions for filtering its series in a spreadsheet.", `<h1>Download historical stock data as CSV</h1><p>Export the selected stock chart without an account or subscription. Choose annual EPS or free cash flow per share, then work with the CSV in Excel, Google Sheets, or another spreadsheet.</p><h2>Choose the company and chart window</h2><ol><li>Open ${companyLink}, or search for a ticker in the <a href="/">chart tool</a>.</li><li>Select Diluted EPS, Basic EPS, or FCF / share. Unavailable metrics are disabled.</li><li>Choose a timeframe, or move the range handles. Review the historical price date and the selected annual observations.</li><li>Click CSV beside the chart. The filename identifies the ticker, metric, and selected start/end dates. Click PNG instead to save the chart as an image.</li></ol><p>The CSV follows the selected chart window and metric. It is a mixed-series export, not a separate download of every column and period in the full company table. Financial periods newer than the last stored price can remain visible in the annual HTML table while lying outside the chart export.</p><h2>Understand the CSV columns</h2><ul><li><strong>series</strong> identifies the kind of observation.</li><li><strong>date</strong> gives the price or fiscal-period date for that row.</li><li><strong>value</strong> contains the observation; an empty value is unavailable, not zero.</li><li><strong>details</strong> supplies context such as the formula multiple or available dividend source and price information.</li></ul><h2>Filter annual EPS or FCF data in a spreadsheet</h2><p>Import the file as comma-separated data and retain the first row as the column headings. Filter series to eps_diluted for diluted EPS, eps_basic for basic EPS, or fcf_per_share for free cash flow per share. Only the selected metric is exported, so change the chart metric and export again if you need another view.</p><p>Other possible series are stooq_adjusted_close, split_only_close when supplied, formula_value, annual_dividend_per_share, and annual_dividend_yield_percent. Sort the filtered dates if you need a chronological annual series. Do not combine price rows and annual financial rows as if they shared a frequency or unit.</p><h2>Keep units and source dates</h2><p>EPS, FCF/share, dividends/share, prices, and formula values use the company's currency per share. Dividend-yield rows use percent. Formula values depend on the selected window and multiple; they are retrospective references, not intrinsic-value estimates.</p><p>CSV financial-metric rows do not include complete filing provenance. Use the company table's SEC links, or the <a href="/data-access/">JSON and Markdown resources</a>, when tracing an observation to its source. Adjusted prices and dividend yields based on them are approximations where validated split-only prices are unavailable.</p><p><a href="/sources/">Check snapshot dates and coverage limits</a> before reusing the data. <a href="/guides/eps-vs-free-cash-flow-per-share/">Learn how EPS and FCF per share differ.</a></p>${credit}`]
